  "security_implications": "The glitch‑abort mechanism ensures that false start bits (e.g., from noise) are rejected and do not produce valid received data. If this mechanism were to fail (due to an incorrect tick_baud_x16 timing, an unstable parity_enable, or a logic bug that prevents idle_d from being asserted), the block could accept a corrupted frame as a legitimate byte. In security‑sensitive applications where UART carries configuration, debug unlock, or other critical data, such a failure could allow an attacker to inject a spurious byte.",
